%N A078441 a(n) begins the first chain of n consecutive positive integers that have equal h-values, where h(k) is the length of the finite sequence k, f(k), f(f(k)), ...., 1 in the Collatz (or 3x + 1) problem. (The earliest "1" is meant.)
%C A078441 Recall that f(n) = n/2 if n is even; = 3n + 1 if n is odd.
%C A078441 From _Omar Said_, Sep 27 2026: (Start)
%C A078441 Garner (1985) conjectured that there are arbitrarily long runs of consecutive integers with the same height. As recalled by Gao (1993), the runs should have the same height, computed with f as here, and the same number of halving steps (A006666); in that form the conjecture implies that a(n) exists for every n.
%C A078441 a(7), a(17), a(25), a(40), a(65), a(98) and a(176) are the starting points of the first seven runs in Table 1 of Gao (1993), which lists the longest run in each range 10^j..10^(j+1); in each of these runs all members also have the same number of halving steps (A006666). The run of 176 integers from a(176) = 722067240 is the longest below 2^30, as found by Wu (1992). (End)
%H A078441 Karl-Heinz Hofmann, <a href="/A078441/b078441.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..255</a>
%H A078441 Guo-Gang Gao, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-365X(93)90240-T">On consecutive numbers of the same height in the Collatz problem</a>, Discrete Math. 112 (1993), 261-267.
%H A078441 Lynn E. Garner, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-365X(85)80020-0">On heights in the Collatz 3n+1 problem</a>, Discrete Math. 55 (1985), 57-64.
%H A078441 Jeffrey C. Lagarias, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0309224">The 3x+1 problem: An annotated bibliography (1963-1999) (sorted by author)</a>, arXiv:math/0309224 [math.NT], 2003-2011 (entries 68, 72 and 188).
%H A078441 Jia-Bang Wu, <a href="https://doi.org/10.13245/j.hust.1992.05.030">A tendency of the proportion of consecutive numbers of the same height in the Collatz problem</a> (in Chinese), J. Huazhong Univ. Sci. Tech. 20 (1992), no. 5, 171-174.
%e A078441 28, 29, 30 is the first chain of three consecutive positive integers n, n+1, n+2 such that h(n) = h(n+1) = h(n+2). Hence a(3)=28.
%t A078441 t = Differences@ Table[Length@ NestWhileList[If[EvenQ@ #, #/2, 3 # + 1] &, n, # != 1 &], {n, 10^5}]; {1}~Join~Table[SequencePosition[t, ConstantArray[0, n - 1]][[1, 1]], {n, 2, 25}] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Sep 14 2016, Version 10.1 *)
%o A078441 (PARI) collatz(x) = {my(t=x, m=0, k=0); while(t>1, if(t%2, t*=3; t++; m++); t>>=1; k++); m+k;}
%o A078441 list(n) = {my(v= vector(n), t0=1, k=0); while(k<n, my(t=t0+1, c=collatz(t0), j); while(collatz(t)==c, t++); j=t-t0; while(k<j && k<n, if(v[k+1]==0, v[k+1]= t0; k++)); t0=t); v;} \\ _V. Barbera_, Sep 28 2026
